12.8 384.7 1018.5 384.5 Q1024.3 384.3 1030.7 381.0 Q1037.1 377.7 1043.2 376.5 Q1049.2 375.2 1053.5 371.0 Q1057.8 366.8 1063.0 364.3 Q1068.2 361.8 1072.5 357.7 Q1076.8 353.6 1079.4 347.5 Q1082.1 341.4 1085.3 336.9 Q1088.5 332.5 1089.4 327.0 Q1090.3 321.5 1090.9 314.7 Q1091.5 308.0 1093.2 303.3 Q1095.0 298.7 1094.4 292.0 Q1093.8 285.4 1092.1 280.9 Q1090.4 276.4 1089.2 271.0 Q1087.9 265.7 1084.1 260.1 Q1080.3 254.5 1078.7 248.9 Q1077.2 243.2 1072.9 239.6 Q1068.5 236.0 1064.2 233.2 Q1059.9 230.4 1054.2 226.2 Q1048.5 221.9 1042.4 219.8 Q1036.4 217.6 1030.8 215.6 Q1025.2 213.5 1020.3 212.1 Q1015.5 210.6 1008.3 211.0 Q1001.2 211.3 991.3 210.3 Q981.5 209.4 970.0 211.1 Q958.6 212.9 947.1 212.7 Q935.7 212.5 923.9 212.6 Q912.1 212.6 901.0 211.1 Q889.9 209.6 879.9 209.6 Q870.0 209.6 859.9 210.2 Q849.9 210.8 836.9 208.8 Q823.9 206.8 814.5 209.1 Q805.0 211.3 792.8 209.9 Q780.7 208.4 771.2 210.7 Q761.8 213.0 748.9 211.8 Q736.0 210.6 725.6 210.4 Q715.2 210.2 704.8 211.7 Q694.4 213.1 684.3 211.1 Q674.1 209.0 662.2 208.5 Q650.4 208.1 639.2 208.5 Q627.9 208.9 616.9 208.0 Q606.0 207.1 594.8 208.8 Q583.7 210.4 572.4 209.8 Q561.1 209.1 551.3 210.2 Q541.5 211.4 530.0 211.4 Q518.4 211.5 507.2 211.0 Q496.0 210.6 484.0 210.8 Q472.0 211.0 462.7 211.6 Q453.3 212.2 442.2 210.7 Q431.1 209.2 419.9 208.4 Q408.7 207.6 397.5 209.1 Q386.3 210.5 374.1 210.8 Q362.0 211.0 352.9 210.0 Q343.9 209.0 331.4 208.7 Q318.8 208.4 308.9 210.1 Q299.0 211.7 287.1 212.0 Q275.3 212.3 265.4 211.8 Q255.6 211.2 244.8 211.1 Q234.1 211.0 222.6 211.2 Q211.1 211.5 199.2 210.3 Q187.3 209.1 176.2 209.0 Q165.2 208.8 155.1 208.1 L145.0 207.3\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" opacity=\".45\"/><path d=\"M1003.4 261.1 Q1002.2 281.8 1004.1 291.9 Q1006.0 301.9 1005.1 311.5 Q1004.2 321.1 1003.9 330.1 L1003.6 339.2\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M118.7 176.4 Q118.1 220.7 119.3 250.8 Q120.4 280.9 119.6 310.9 Q118.8 341.0 118.6 372.6 Q118.3 404.3 119.8 415.0 L121.4 425.7\" stroke-width=\"2\" opacity=\".7\"/></g><circle cx=\"1004\" cy=\"255\" r=\"5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></svg><figcaption>The diaulos: the race of two stades, out, round the turning post, and back. The two lines also read as a double pipe.</figcaption></figure><p>The country's new supercomputer, inaugurated at Lavrio, has been named \"Daedalus\". It is the name of a craftsman. The mythical Daedalus was the Athenian who knew how to work matter until it became something else, and the word has kept this from the beginning: in Greek, <i>daidallō</i> means to work something with art. Everything he made was a passage. The labyrinth was corridors that held inside whoever entered. The wings were a way out, over the sea. The same hand made both, and what tells them apart does not lie in the making. It lies in the relation between the one who crosses them and the place he wants to reach.</p><p>The maker's name says what was made. It does not say how one is to stand within what was made. That second thing has another word, very close to the first: <i>diaulos</i>.</p><p>The place helps the difference show. In antiquity, Lavrio gave silver. The building that now houses the machine once gave electric power. First metal, then energy, now computation, and through computation, language. Each time the place produces something that does not stay there. The silver became coin and travelled into hands that never saw the mines. The current became light in houses that never knew the power station. Language, however, when it comes out of such a machine, does something the others did not: it returns to the one who spoke.</p><p>This is the <i>diaulos</i> in its ancient sense: the race of two stades, in which the runner reaches the far mark, turns, and comes back to the starting point. He finishes where he began, but he is no longer the same. Written dialogue with a language model moves in the same way. The sentence leaves, passes through the mass of recorded language, through what was written by people we will never know, and comes back. The machine at Lavrio is the physical body of such a course, made of silicon, cooling water and megawatts. The name \"Daedalus\" looks at this body and honours its construction. The name \"Diaulos\" looks at what passes through it.</p><p>The word has a second root, the <i>aulos</i>: the pipe through which breath passes and becomes sound. A <i>diaulos</i> is a double pipe. Here something appears that the craftsman need not say: the instrument has no breath of its own. It sounds when someone blows into it. The language the machine gives out is the breath of the people who wrote it, kept, together with the breath of the one who now asks and sets it moving. An <i>aulos</i> without a mouth is a tube. It becomes an instrument the moment someone plays.</p><p>Every <i>diaulos</i> has two ends, and the course exists to be run. Great works open slowly. First the construction is finished, then it is settled who may enter and how, and only with time does the course fill with runners. The name given to a work shows where on this path it stands. \"Daedalus\" is the name of the moment the work is completed. \"Diaulos\" is the name of the moment it begins to be used.</p><p>Perhaps, then, every construction of language has two names. One is given by its maker. The other is earned by use, and no one writes it on it. Daedalus made the wings. The flight becomes a <i>diaulos</i> when someone flies and returns.</p>"
